To allow null values for a date field in Spring MVC, you can take advantage of the `DateFormatter` in combination with a custom converter or by configuring your controllers to handle empty values appropriately. 

### Option 1: Custom Converter

You can create a custom converter that can handle both the date parsing and the case when the input is empty (i.e., the field is left blank). Here’s how to do it:

1. **Create a Custom Converter**:

```java
import org.springframework.core.convert.converter.Converter;
import org.springframework.util.StringUtils;

import java.text.ParseException;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.util.Date;

public class StringToDateConverter implements Converter<String, Date> {
    private final SimpleDateFormat dateFormat;

    public StringToDateConverter(String dateFormat) {
        this.dateFormat = new SimpleDateFormat(dateFormat);
    }

    @Override
    public Date convert(String source) {
        if (!StringUtils.hasText(source)) {
            return null; // Return null for empty strings
        }
        try {
            return dateFormat.parse(source);
        } catch (ParseException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid date format: " + source);
        }
    }
}
```

2. **Register the Custom Converter**:

Update your `conversionService` bean to include this converter.

```xml
<beans:bean id="conversionService"
    class="org.springframework.format.support.FormattingConversionServiceFactoryBean">
    <beans:property name="converters">
        <beans:list>
            <beans:bean class="com.yourpackage.StringToDateConverter">
                <beans:constructor-arg value="dd/MM/yyyy"/>
            </beans:bean>
        </beans:list>
    </beans:property>
    <beans:property name="formatters">
        <beans:set>
            <beans:bean id="dateFormatter" class="org.springframework.format.datetime.DateFormatter">
                <beans:constructor-arg>
                    <beans:value>dd/MM/yyyy</beans:value>
                </beans:constructor-arg>
            </beans:bean>
        </beans:set>
    </beans:property>
</beans:bean>
```

### Option 2: Update Your Controller

Ensure that if a date is not provided and the binding occurs, you handle it gracefully in your controller. For example:

```java
@PostMapping("/submit")
public String submitForm(@ModelAttribute YourModel model, BindingResult result) {
    Date date = model.getDate();
    if (date == null) {
        // handle null date scenario, e.g., set a default value or show an error
    }
    // Process your model...
    return "resultPage";
}
```

### Notes:
- With the custom converter approach, empty strings will simply return `null`.
- Ensure that you handle null values in your business logic when the date is expected to be nullable.
- Be cautious about how you use the date in other parts of your application to avoid `NullPointerExceptions`.

With this setup, your date field should correctly handle null/empty values while still formatting and converting valid date input.

